          "output_excerpt": "ing the four general procedures above. Alternatively, append the same guidance as a short additive block in the system prompt.\n\n**Why it preserves existing capabilities:**\n- The skill is **additive and opt-in**: the loader only appends a catalog line to the system prompt and exposes `read_skill`; the already-solved direct-comparison task's reasoning path is untouched.\n- It is a **prompt/skill-layer change**, not a change to shared control flow in `agent.py`, `workspace.py`, or the reader — so no middleware ordering, tool dispatch, or answer-finalization behavior is affected.\n- It contains **no task IDs, no answers, no evaluation edits, and no credentials** — only genre-level reasoning guidance (hop decomposition, alias resolution, constraint expansion, answer normalization), so it generalizes to any multi-hop bridge question rather than the three measured ones.\n- It is **narrowly scoped**: it cannot fail closed, since a missing or inactive skill degrades to exactly the current behavior.\n\n**Residual uncertainty:** The failure diagnosis is inferred from the dataset/context shape and the fact that both unsolved items are bridge-type with synonym/derived-reference hops; I did not read the per-task model outputs, so the actual failure mode (wrong surface entity vs. truncation vs. premature stop under `max_turns: 3`) is unconfirmed. Any implementation should be validated across repeated runs, and the 3-turn budget may itself be the binding constraint for bridge questions.\n</DESIGN>"
        }
